WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.980
(dramatic music)
(audience applauding)

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:04.980
(audience cheering)

00:00:12.969 --> 00:00:13.860
- Thank you.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:15.750
Thank you, thank you so much.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:17.520
Your lovely comments, Glenn,

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:20.490
just, I'm so touched by what you said.

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:21.510
Thank you.

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:25.740
Oh my, talk about a change
in pace, what editors can do.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:27.630
Whew! (laughs)

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:30.750
Now, (breathes deeply) ah.

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:32.403
You always say to breathe, right?

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:34.380
- [Audience Member] Breathe!

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:37.440
- I first met Glenn at the start

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:40.920
of the ensemble cast
rehearsal for "The Big Chill,"

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:44.580
on location in Beaufort, South Carolina,

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.980
before principal photography started.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:49.473
Can you believe such a crazy notion?

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:54.630
Larry Kasdan asked me to
attend the actors' rehearsals

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:56.940
where I became a fly on the wall,

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:01.170
watching and listening
to the actors' process.

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:03.960
Glenn was responsible for teaching me

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:05.913
the key lesson of editing,

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:08.610
to interpret the actors' work

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:11.520
within a dynamic of the ensemble

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:14.580
to heighten and hone the
experience incrementally

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.410
for the audience.

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:19.140
I was able to watch her hard work,

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:22.800
the distillation of
character and relationships

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.923
grow naturally without effort.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:28.890
And for that, Glenn,

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:31.800
for that fundamental lesson of simplicity,

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.260
my north star for over 40 years,

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.818
I am eternally grateful.

00:01:36.818 --> 00:01:38.763
(audience applauding)
- Woo!

00:01:40.332 --> 00:01:42.308
(Carol chuckling)

00:01:42.308 --> 00:01:44.360
It was an extraordinary experience.

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:49.380
Well, this is the beginning
my adventure in film,

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:52.350
back as a kid in rural Oklahoma,

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:55.923
where I grew up in a
small town on Route 66.

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:02.030
In the 1940s, my hometown
had three walk-in theaters,

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:05.790
the Coleman, a beautiful movie palace,

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:08.070
now beautifully restored,

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:12.000
and the now-shuttered
Ottawa and the Glory B,

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.170
where my sister and I

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:17.520
attended the kiddie matinee every Saturday

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.130
with our little outfits,
our Western outfits,

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:24.060
cowboy boots, hat, jeans,

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:26.640
and two shiny quarters in our pocket

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:28.560
to cover the price of admission

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:32.790
for three hours of
uninterrupted entertainment,

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:36.870
starting with 20 minutes of cartoons,

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:41.870
two serials, usually episodes
of "Hopalong Cassidy,"

00:02:42.127 --> 00:02:44.877
"Flash Gordon," or "Captain America,"

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:49.260
the Western, of the
singing cowboy variety,

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:54.260
starring Roy Rogers and
Dale Evans or Gene Autry,

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:55.923
and once in a while,

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:01.880
a movie about my favorite
Ozark hillbillies,

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:03.360
Ma and Pa Kettle.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:05.670
- Woo!
(audience laughing)

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:07.773
- At an Oscar event, way!
- Woo!

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:14.600
- Kids without adults had
unbridled freedom to laugh

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:20.460
and shout and make believe in total chaos.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:22.263
It was fantastic. I loved it.

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:28.080
Flash forward from Miami,
Oklahoma, not Miami, Florida,

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:31.500
to Paris, where I spent
my junior year abroad.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:34.680
When I arrived, I barely
spoke the language

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:39.660
and I needed a shortcut for
learning colloquial French.

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:40.923
So here's the secret.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:43.713
Watch en version originale,

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:48.723
an American film with French subtitles.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:55.900
And not only that, I got to discover

00:03:57.829 --> 00:04:00.279
the classic Western at
the Cinematheque Francais,

00:04:01.357 --> 00:04:05.767
"Beautiful World of John
Ford," at a retrospective.

00:04:05.767 --> 00:04:09.667
"She Wore a Yellow Ribbon," "Ford Apache,"

00:04:09.667 --> 00:04:12.870
"My Darling Clementine," "Stagecoach,"

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:13.947
and "The Searchers."

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:18.510
My adventure in film
continued that same summer

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:22.593
when I met a lanky student in
Florence, Italy, John Bailey.

00:04:23.447 --> 00:04:24.728
(audience applauding)

00:04:24.728 --> 00:04:26.343
(laughs) Thank you.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:31.860
Little did I know that
John would change my life,

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:34.380
lure me to Los Angeles,

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:37.980
where we would marry and
share a life in film,

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:42.630
working our way through the
ranks, learning on the job,

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:45.000
helping and supporting each other.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.283
We were lucky to work
on 12 films together.

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:51.570
I thank Lawrence Kasdan, Jonathan Demme,

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:52.950
and Ken Kwapis,

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:56.670
who understood the value
of our dual collaboration,

00:04:56.670 --> 00:05:00.990
and I thank them deeply
and forever for that gift.

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:01.961
Larry. (laughs)

00:05:01.961 --> 00:05:05.128
(audience applauding)

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:08.380
There are always perplexing moments

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:12.000
during the editorial process.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:16.260
We had one such crucial moment
during the editing of "E.T."

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:21.180
when we suffered the
equivalent of writers block.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:23.163
I guess we called it editors blank.

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:27.870
Steven had the brilliant idea

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:32.487
to cook Matzo Brei for
the entire editorial crew.

00:05:32.487 --> 00:05:33.320
(audience laughing)

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:36.277
Delicious. Problem solved.

00:05:36.277 --> 00:05:38.010
(audience laughing)

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:41.730
I promised I would never
disclose his recipe,

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:43.870
his secret recipe for success

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:47.130
but if you take The New York Times,

00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:48.810
look at the cooking app,

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:50.717
I think you'll find the secret there.

00:05:50.717 --> 00:05:53.490
(audience laughing)

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:56.100
when I think about the privilege I had

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:59.253
to work on one of the most
entertaining, enduring,

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:04.080
and endearing films of all time,

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:05.497
I'm frequently asked,

00:06:05.497 --> 00:06:08.967
"Why do you think 'E.T.'
is a perennial favorite?"

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:12.810
There are so many
reasons, but fundamentally

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:17.373
it's its powerful story about
tolerance for the other.

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:24.000
Elliott, Michael, and
Gertie learn to trust

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:26.013
and love the alien among them.

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:28.323
With a little humidity.

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:31.710
Excuse me. (laughs)
(audience laughing)

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:34.950
With a little humility and patience,

00:06:34.950 --> 00:06:39.843
we too can find our common
humanity in uncommon places.

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:44.943
This Oscar is the Everest
moment of my adventure in film,

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:47.940
a dream fulfilled,

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:49.650
and I wanna thank the Governors

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:52.530
of the Academy of Motion
Picture Arts and Sciences.

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:54.130
I accept this beautiful Oscar

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:58.263
as a highlight of my life in film.

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:00.990
I accept it for all editors

00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:03.813
who toil in the darkness
of an editing room,

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:09.660
weighing each cut, making infinite choices

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:12.870
to create a unique, believable world

00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:14.313
borne of the imagination.

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:20.300
And most of all, I want to
thank John, my dear Johnny.

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:22.371
Thank you.

00:07:22.371 --> 00:07:25.039
(audience applauding)
- Woo!

00:07:25.039 --> 00:07:27.872
(dramatic music)

